== This lecture:

We're going to learn context:
- We're going to see the unique requirements and motivations behind 3D graphics development, framed in their historical context.
- We're going to learn how 3D graphics turned into a radically different paradigm than ordinary software development.
- We're going to learn why that matters: the 3D graphics pipeline.

== The pipeline

This pipeline concept is incredibly important.

We don't program 3D apps the same way as other apps. Everything you have learned on your computer science journey up to now will be useful, but you will need to go beyond basic synchronous programming.

But it seems very strange at first, so I want to give you some context about the field and some ancient history to motivate it.

== Computer Graphics

As a field, computer graphics is the study of generating images with computers. That's it.

This seems like a very simple definition, and it is, but the field is anything but simple. 

Humans who can see often use it as their primary sense: most of us are very visual creatures. Therefore, we are very demanding of the images that we look at.

Specifically, we are demanding in two ways...

== What we demand of computer graphics
+ We demand that the images have certain qualities (realism, artistic style, clarity, etc.)
+ We demand that they be generated quickly.

Let's talk first about the qualities we want...

== Kinds of generated images

There are many applications for computer graphics. The application determines what qualities we want the generated images to have:
- Scientific visualization: the key is on expressing mathematical concepts to humans quickly. So images need to be clear and understandable.
- Simulation: realism is important here.
- Art (including games): creative expression is important

These are jobs that exist. Someone has to program the graphics card to achieve the desired visual effects. Look for job titles such as "graphics engineer" or "technical artist".

